In article <16697@uqcspe.cs.uq.oz.au> warwick@cs.uq.oz.au writes:
>mtc@henry.ece.cmu.edu (Magnetic Technology Center) writes:
>
>>outdated concept: wand of wishing
>>    replacing this one item opens the door for an array of other
>>    interesting things (sorry, no details at present).  the WOW cannot
>>    really be justified on its own merits or as a link to NetHack's past.
>
>I agree.

No wand of wishing?  How about a compromise:  No randomly generated
wands of wishing.  There has to be some special reward in the castle
(like there is in The Mines, the Quest [two of them there], Ft. Ludios
[well, lots of gold anyway], Vlad's Tower, and the Wizlevel).  By the
time characters get therethe WoW is just about the only thing that
would excite them.

>All objects should have a WISHABLE flags, and most things should not be
>wishable.  

I agree with the general idea of WISHABLE flags, but not with your
proportions.  Only a select few items (the most powerful ones in the
game) should be unwishable.  If the best thing you can get with a wish
is +0 plate mail, wishing will cease to be worthwhile at all.

>No artifact wishing.

In the case of quest artifacts, I agree.  I _might_ agree in the case
of regular artifacts, but the game would have to be changed:  The
early midgame (which the general consensus seems to be is the toughest
part--its certainly the part _I_ usually end up dying in.  I'm talking
right after you've finished the Gnomish Mines, but before you're
strong enough to go on your Quest) is, I think, now configured so that
you're _expected_ to have lots of nifty stuff--more than altars can
usually give you (I, personally, have never gotten a character who
didn't get a wish past character level 10--of course I'm not a
particularly skilled player).  Now, if the game were changed so that
mid-level monsters were much wimpier (mumaks, dragons, giants etc. not
showing up until a bit later, for example), chrysknives were made more
powerful, and nifty mundane objects were more readily available, I
might be persuaded.

This would have one particularly nice feature:  more diversity between
the classes (which we've almost all agreed is A Good Thing).  It
would, on this new system, be hard to find even a 25th level mage
weilding Mjollnir--if they got it from their god, it would be a wild
coincidence!  Just about all of them would probably have Magicbane as
their primary weapon instead.  Similarly, Barbarians would probably
have to make do with Cleaver unless/until they became EoB--leading to
a drastically different playing style than say Knights, who will have
lower hitpoints but a better weapon.

>Wishes only from Djinies, and make magic lamps indistinguishable from
>oil lamps, except that they never run out.  (ie. calling oil-lamps
>"foobar" would also label magic lamps as "foobar").  Reasoning?  It
>isn't the LAMP that is magic, it's the CONTENTS.

Fair 'nuff, except for the first part [I'd want to keep the castle
WoW, and the off-chance that you'll get a wish from a fountain at
early levels generates some interesting risk-taking].  How about by
identify scrolls?  Or by the "how much will the Shopkeeper sell it
for" test? 



